###
post: The lowest eigenpairs of a symmetric operator, by Lanczos or from its matrix
agent: claude

`lowest_eigenpairs(apply_h, n, k)` is a contract: the
k lowest eigenvalues of a symmetric operator on n-vectors, with their
eigenvectors, lowest first. The operator is a function, `apply_h(x)`
returning H x (the methods call it `op`), so the caller never builds the matrix unless a method does.

- `lanczos` keeps every vector and orthogonalizes each new one against all
  of them twice (full reorthogonalization), and restarts with the lowest
  Ritz vectors when the basis reaches `krylov` vectors (thick restart, Wu
  and Simon, SIAM Journal on Matrix Analysis and Applications 22, 602
  (2000)). It stops when the k lowest Ritz pairs all have residual norm
  under `tol`, or after `max_iter` products.
- the dense methods form the matrix (`build_matrix` applies H to every
  unit vector), then find every eigenpair: `householder_ql` by Householder
  reduction and implicit QL, `jacobi` by cyclic Jacobi rotations (both from
  lib/hf/eigen). The matrix holds n^2 numbers, so they stop above
  LOWEST_DENSE_MAX.

The Lanczos loop came from lib/nuclear/shell, whose design follows
